e of Versailles during the reign of Louis XIV."}, {"sourceSlug": "alphonse-henri-of-lorraine-count-of-harcourt", "sourceName": "Alphonse Henri of Lorraine, Count of Harcourt", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "order-of-the-holy-spirit", "targetName": "Order of the Holy Spirit", "context": "Alphonse Henri was inducted as a Chevalier into the prestigious Order of the Holy Spirit, reflecting his high noble status in 1688."}, {"sourceSlug": "alphonse-henri-of-lorraine-count-of-harcourt", "sourceName": "Alphonse Henri of Lorraine, Count of Harcourt", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "house-of-lorraine", "targetName": "House of Lorraine", "context": "Through his descendants, Alphonse Henri helped transmit the lineage and prestige of the Harcourt branch of the House of Lorraine into the 18th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Birthplace and primary residence"}, {"name": "Versailles, France", "role": "Major courtly presence"}, {"name": "Harcourt, France", "role": "Family titular domain"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Alphonse Henri of Lorraine, Count of Harcourt", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "alphonse-henri-of-lorraine-count-of-harcourt"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3611162", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonse_Henri,_Count_of_Harcourt"], "tags": ["France", "Political Leaders", "Europe", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 4, "significanceNarrative": "Alphonse Henri's historical importance stems from his role in maintaining the prestige of a major noble family within the evolving French court, illustrating the aristocratic transition under Louis XIV. His life provides insight into the social dynamics and dynastic continuity of the Ancien Régime.", "significanceCategory": "local"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-19T08:16:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44485681343", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Alphonse Henri de Lorraine was a member of the House of Lorraine and Count of Harcourt.", "newValue": "Alphonse Henri Charles de Lorraine (1648 – January 19, 1719) was a prominent French nobleman and courtier, inheriting the title of Count of Harcourt within the influential House of Lorraine. His life spanned the zenith of Louis XIV's reign, a period characterized by the absolute monarchy and the elaborate social rituals of Versailles. While not a central figure in military campaigns or high politics, Alphonse Henri's significance lies in his embodiment of the aristocratic ideal of the era, maintaining his family's prestige and influence through strategic marriages and a visible presence at court, thereby contributing to the intricate web of power that sustained the Bourbon dynasty.

Born in Paris, Alphonse Henri was the son of François Marie de Lorraine, Prince de Lillebonne, and Anne de Lorraine. He solidified his family's position through his marriage to Marie Françoise de Brancas (1650–1725) in 1667, a union that brought considerable wealth and connections. Though he held no major governmental or military command, his status as a Prince étranger—a title signifying a foreign princely lineage recognized at the French court—afforded him high rank and privileges. He was admitted into the prestigious Order of the Holy Spirit (Chevalier de l'Ordre du Saint-Esprit), a testament to his standing among the highest nobility.

Alphonse Henri's life reflected the evolving role of the French aristocracy under Louis XIV, transitioning from independent powerbrokers to courtiers whose influence stemmed from proximity to the monarch. His lineage, tracing back to the ancient Duchy of Lorraine, ensured his family's continued prominence, even as their direct political power waned. His children and grandchildren continued to serve the French crown, extending the House of Lorraine's legacy well into the 18th century and beyond, illustrating the enduring power of dynastic continuity in the Ancien Régime."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-19T08:16:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44485681343", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1648-08-14, France"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1648-08-14, France"}], "newValue": ["The inherent prestige and ancient lineage of the House of Lorraine provided Alphonse Henri with significant social standing at the French court.", "The consolidation of power under Louis XIV compelled high nobility to seek influence through courtly presence rather than independent regional authority.", "Strategic marital alliances, particularly with Marie Françoise de Brancas, secured his family's wealth and connections within the French aristocracy."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-19T08:16:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44485681343", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped political institutions and governance practices", "type": "Idea", "year": "1719-01-19"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1719-01-19"}], "newValue": ["Alphonse Henri's continued presence at Versailles reinforced the House of Lorraine's enduring influence and prestige among the Fre